<Base
  title="FleetPilot — Christopher Uzochukwu"
  description="A simulated fleet reconciler in Go: desired state in PostgreSQL, a per-instance lock, bounded retries, and a fake in-process provider."
>
  <h1>FleetPilot</h1>
  <p class="dateline">Christopher Uzochukwu · 29 October 2026 · Africa/Lagos</p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot/actions/workflows/test.yml">
      <img
        class="badge"
        alt="FleetPilot test workflow status"
        src="https://img.shields.io/github/actions/workflow/status/chrisuzor/fleetpilot/test.yml?branch=main"
        width="88"
        height="20"
      />
    </a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Public repository: <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot">github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot</a>.
    Architecture: <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot/blob/main/docs/architecture.md">docs/architecture.md</a>.
    The longer note is <a href="/writing/fleetpilot-reconciliation/">Desired state, one lock, and a short retry</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Desired state and actual state drift. Two workers can both see that a machine is missing and
    both try to create it. A provider error can also be retried until that name is stuck. FleetPilot
    is a small Go control plane that keeps those two problems in one loop.
  </p>

  <h2 id="architecture">Loop</h2>
  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 360 470" role="img" aria-labelledby="fleet-diagram-title fleet-diagram-desc">
      <title id="fleet-diagram-title">FleetPilot reconcile loop</title>
      <desc id="fleet-diagram-desc">
        Desired state in PostgreSQL goes to the reconciler, then a job queue, then a worker. The
        worker calls the fake provider and writes an audit log. PostgreSQL also stores the queue
        and the audit log.
      </desc>
