    <figcaption>
      The reconciler compares desired rows with the fake provider and enqueues one job per
      difference. It does not call create, update, or delete. The worker takes the name, applies
      the job, and the audit log records the outcome.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The lock is a PostgreSQL row. The worker inserts the instance name into
    <code>instance_locks</code> if needed and selects it <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>. A
    second applier does not wait and does not call the provider. That miss does not count as an
    attempt. Provider failures wait 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms, and 800 ms. The fifth provider failure
    marks the job dead. Create and delete are safe to repeat, so a replay does not apply the same
    change twice. A rollout updates one name at a time and pauses on an unhealthy check or a dead
    job. It is a rolling update, not a canary. Routes under <code>/v1</code> read
    <code>X-FleetPilot-Role</code> with the value <code>reader</code> or <code>operator</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it simulates</h2>
  <p>
    The provider is fake and in-process. Image, size, and region are labels. Reconcile, the lock,
    the retry budget, health checks, and a one-name rolling update all run against that record.
    Kubernetes manifests and an OpenTofu module describe a local layout. Traces go to stdout.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not do</h2>
  <p>
    It does not boot a machine or call a cloud API. The role header is not a credential. GitHub
    Actions runs <code>go test -race ./...</code>, lint, and a local image build. That test job
    does not start PostgreSQL, and it does not apply the module or push the image. There is no live
    traffic.
  </p>

  <h2>Tests</h2>
  <p>From the repository:</p>
  <pre><code>go test -race ./...</code></pre>
  <p>
    The latest run on main completed successfully. Unit tests use the in-memory queue and lock and
    the fake provider.
  </p>

  <h2>Example request</h2>
  <p>
    Compose listens on <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>. Create stores desired state. Reconcile enqueues
    the difference. The worker applies it.
  </p>
  <pre><code>curl -sS -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/instances \
  -H 'X-FleetPilot-Role: operator' \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '&#123;"name":"web-1","image":"ubuntu-24.04","size":"small","region":"local"&#125;'</code></pre>
  <pre><code>curl -sS -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/reconcile \
  -H 'X-FleetPilot-Role: operator'</code></pre>

